			<div class="content" id="post-content-4821067">
									

<p>Note: I'm not particularly experienced with this kind of thing, so take what I say with a grain of salt.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Crit&nbsp;Time!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Crit&nbsp;Time!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>General Thoughts</strong>: Overall, it's pretty okay. It does a good job of explaining the anomaly (at least, what can be explained about it), but it never really left me with things to think about. Some of the Research Logs explained things that you could leave the reader to think about themselves. A lot of what makes an SCP good is how its abilities affect the world around it, not really the abilities themselves. The effects on the world can make up a central narrative. However, how the anomaly affects the world around it should leave a sense of wonder. As in, &quot;what benefits could this provide if used correctly, and what problems could it cause while abused?&quot;. Leaving the reader to think about the possibilities can keep them engaged. If you straight up tell us some possibilities, well, that sense of wonder is lost, and so is the engagement. We don't want to be spoon-fed information, we want to find that information ourselves. In summary, try to <em>show</em>, not <em>tell</em>.</p>
<p><strong>Line-By-Line:</strong> Please note that these have not been listed in any particular order.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5181 is to be stored at Site-14 in a five meter by five meter by five meter (5x5x5)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, from what I can see currently, this measurement of 5x5x5 meters does not matter at all. If it does matter, answer this question: &quot;What would happen if I put it in a 4x4x4 meter room?&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If it is possible, we might have found an SCP that can turn any element into another, making supply issues a thing of the past. Why it could even be used to reverse the effects of climate change! The possibilities are potentially endless.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is one of the lines that influenced the start of the crit; it just didn't leave me to wonder about the possibilities. Also, this doesn't add anything to the article. It's hollow information that doesn't go anywhere, and it feels like filler. There's no &quot;action&quot; that comes out of this part if you know what I mean. Not saying you can't include the Research Logs, just put actual content in there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Using this SCP to resolve any material supply issues the Foundation has is moot now, with the exception of any SCP that requires gas to contain them. That's something I suppose. There's still a very good chance that we can use this device to undo all the damage we've done in regards to climate change though</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ditto.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is horrifying. It can convert the carbon dioxide polluting our atmosphere into oxygen, but we would be unable to breathe it. The discovery report hints that this thing could have been spewing carbon dioxide into the atmosphere for years, that's millions of cubic meters of CO2 in our atmosphere that will never disappear, that plants will never be able to turn into oxygen, that we can never fix. My god.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ditto. Also, &quot;This is horrifying&quot; is way too basic. If your character is that simple, then that's fine, but try to practice putting ideas into people's heads without stating the idea directly. Come to think of it, that's the theme of this critique, just a matter of showing and not telling.</p>
<div class="blockquote">
<p><strong>REVISED CONTAINMENT PROCEDURES</strong></p>
<p>SCP-5181 is to be contained at Site-14 in a five meter by five meter by five meter (5x5x5) airtight concrete containment chamber with lead-lined steel interior plating and an internal atmospheric cycling system with an adequate supply of a natural oxygen/nitrogen mix. Two robotic arms with built in atmospheric sensors and air sampling equipment are to be present inside the chamber, one for plugging and unplugging SCP-5181 from the wall outlet installed and another for remote control of the objects LED screen interface, both of which are controlled by the remote observation booth via computer. Maintenance of the chamber is to be conducted once every two weeks by on-site technicians using proper Hazardous Materials Suits equipped with a Self-Contained Breathing Apperatus upon their persons. Internal atmosphere must be purged of all anomalous gases before maintenance can begin. No other personnel are permitted within SCP-5181's containment cell at any time. Negative numbers are locked out of the computer interface and are unable to be inputted. SCP-5181 must remain unpowered at all times until permission for further testing is granted.</p>
</div>
<br />
More of a formatting thing than anything else, but please make containment related things as close to the top of the article as you can. Remember, people are going to be reading these in case something bad happens, and they shouldn't be looking through the article to find containment related things. It should be one of the first things they see.</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Afterword:</strong> There may be a good amount of things I missed (hell, maybe a lot of things I missed, I'm still new to this myself, as I said), but overall, it's starting to look good. Just keep getting feedback :)</p>
<p><strong>Edit:</strong> Oh, and I know it's a provisional designation, but you might not want to use an SCP number before you've posted it. Not strictly necessary though because, as mentioned in the draft, it's just a provisional designation.</p>

