<blockquote>
<p>didn't want to buck the trend</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not saying trends are always a bad thing (hell, the SCP article format is a trend in and of itself), but when the trend is almost always something that is not ideal, you probably want to stay away from that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also helped for calculating some math in regards to the amount of time it takes for this skip to alter the air in a room or add anomalous gas to the atmosphere, since 5 is a prime number and all.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;<em>There was math involved?</em> In all seriousness, I don't see anything in your description that contributes to the math involved here. There was no mention of how fast the fan converts things (not in terms of time, but in terms of how fast the air flows). Yes, the test logs give us a timeframe of how long it takes for the fan to finish converting air, but it gives us an expression we can solve, not an equation we can check. From what I can gather (and definitely correct me if I'm wrong), you were using the room measurements and the airflow to determine the amount of time it would take for it to convert air. However, the airflow is never mentioned, so the math you did literally doesn't matter because there's nothing to be wrong about (you know, cause it's not &quot;solved&quot; in the article yet). If you've forgotten to include the airflow, be sure to include it in your description so that your Special Containment Procedures make sense. Thanks for clearing up your reasons for including the measurements though, I get it now (though I'm still a little bit apprehensive considering that these are Special <em>Containment</em> Procedures and are not meant for testing, but you do you lol).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I have seen articles have revised containment procedures listed at the bottom sometimes accompanied by the words 'pending approval' and that was the thing I was aiming for with that.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sort of the same thing as the trend idea I mentioned above, except it's not a bad thing. There's a reason those &quot;Pending Approval&quot; procedures are at the bottom. They're not in action yet. Special Containment Procedures are there for something that you <em>should</em> do in case of an emergency. However, the &quot;Pending Approval&quot; procedures are just that. Pending approval. It's good to keep them in mind, but they aren't strictly necessary. I don't see (or rather, it's not clarified) why your revised containment procedures aren't strictly necessary at the time of the article. If it's pending approval, clarify that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also I was worried that the revision may give something away in regards to the story, for now they stay where they are but I'm 50/50 about moving them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Let's talk about the foreshadowing aspect of Special Containment Procedures. Generally, good containment procedures should give you a general idea of what the anomaly is like. You have a fire anomaly? Use fire-resistant materials. It gives you an idea of what the anomaly is without spoiling it. I'm not great at foreshadowing myself, but doing it right shouldn't give away your story too badly.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Your critique here was super helpful with that, and the lines you have pointed out as superfluous or lacklustre have been altered with your feedback. I'm hoping it's an improvement over the original!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No problem! And yes, I've just looked over it, and it's made improvements over the original. The one I can most easily point out the improvements on (that isn't to say nothing else improved, this is just the one I can most easily explain) is the one about some of the oxygen in the atmosphere being non-breathable. That part worked <em>really</em> well considering how the SCP was discovered. This is what you should continue doing, giving the reader pieces of the puzzle and having them connect them.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall though, nice revision! Keep getting feedback and you'll have a successful article in no time.</p>

							</div>

								
